>`BT2b1c0e`FTff6e00Past Meetings

`FTc8a06a`!What we've talked about`!`f

>>`BT1a1109`FTc8a06aD-STAR: Digital Voice, Data, and Callsign Routing

`FT9a7040September 1, 2026 — Mark Noble KE8BZW/WQWJ432`f

D-STAR — Digital Smart Technologies for Amateur Radio — is an open digital voice and data standard developed by the Japan Amateur Radio League. Rather than putting your voice on the air as analog FM, it sends a digital bitstream with a low-speed data channel riding alongside it, and it routes contacts by callsign instead of by frequency. Call another operator by their callsign and the network figures out which repeater to deliver you to.

Mark introduced D-STAR from the ground up: what it is, where it came from, and how it compares to analog FM and to the other digital modes you'll run into on the air. He covered what it takes to get started — a radio, a registration, and either a repeater in range or a hotspot of your own — along with what the internet-linked reflectors make possible once you're on.

>>`BT1a1109`FTc8a06aGoing Vertical: Signals from the Sky

`FT9a7040August 4, 2026 — Tom Graham WX8TOM`f

Last year's CodeMash balloon launch flew all the way to Alunda, Sweden. Tom explored the Raspberry Pi PICO and the high altitude balloons that carry it to near space at over 100,000 feet.

He showed how PICO boards and recycled radiosondes can be programmed to send telemetry that gets received and decoded by SDRs. The data from these flying devices is uploaded to the internet, which means you can follow and analyze balloon flights from anywhere — no special equipment required. He also showed how Docker works as a portable compiler to build and run the tracking code.

>>`BT1a1109`FTc8a06aYou've Probably Never Heard of the Reticulum Network

`FT9a7040July 7, 2026 — Guy Royse W8GUY`f

Reticulum is a cryptography-based networking stack that runs over almost any hardware you can imagine — LoRa, WiFi, Ethernet, old school packet radio, and plain old serial cables. No central infrastructure. No single point of control. Mesh everything.

Guy covered the technical details: what Reticulum is, how the protocol works, and how packets, routing, and destinations create a self-healing mesh of encrypted and uncentralizable greatness where you own your address. He poked at the command-line utilities that ship with it and the third-party software that runs over it — rnstatus, MeshChat, and NomadNet — along with code for building apps of your own.

The session finished hands-on, standing up a working Reticulum network with everyone in the room over WiFi, Ethernet, and LoRa.

`[ Slides & Materials`https://github.com/coredotradio/meetings/tree/main/meetings/2026-07]

>>`BT1a1109`FTc8a06aAn Introduction to DMR as an IP Transport

`FT9a7040June 2, 2026 — Alex Barbur`f

Digital Mobile Radio (DMR) is best known as a digital voice protocol for commercial and amateur two-way radio, but it's also a capable bearer for low-bandwidth IP traffic — useful when you need applications in the field to talk to each other without cellular, WiFi, or internet infrastructure.

Alex introduced the DMR protocol from the ground up: what it is, how it differs from analog FM and other digital modes, and the basics of time slots, talkgroups, and color codes. From there, he walked through how DMR can be used to move IP packets between devices in the field, what the practical bandwidth and latency look like, and what kinds of applications actually work over that pipe.

`[ Slides & Materials`https://github.com/coredotradio/meetings/tree/main/meetings/2026-06]

>>`BT1a1109`FTc8a06aRadio Signal Analysis Using SDRs and OpenWebRX+

`FT9a7040May 5, 2026 — Scott McCrory KE8SAU/WRML487`f

Software-Defined Radio has never been more accessible — or more capable. In this session, Scott explored how a modest hardware investment and open-source software can turn a browser into a full-featured radio monitoring station reachable from anywhere on the internet.

The presentation opened with a live demo of a working remote SDR installation, accessible directly from a browser — no plugins, no special client software required. From there, we walked through the complete hardware and software stack, covered the key installation and configuration steps, and discussed signal path considerations and antenna choices, along with their real-world limitations.

The bulk of the session dove into practical signal monitoring across a wide swath of the spectrum: APRS packet traffic, ADS-B flight tracking, VHF and UHF activity, GMRS, CB, and HF — and what OpenWebRX+ makes visible that used to require dedicated hardware for each band.

>>`BT1a1109`FTc8a06aWiFi HaLow: Introduction and Practical Use

`FT9a7040April 7, 2026 — Alex Barbur`f

WiFi HaLow (802.11ah) is a low-power, long-range variant of WiFi designed for IoT and embedded applications. Operating in the sub-GHz band, it trades raw speed for dramatically better range and penetration — making it a compelling option for sensors, remote monitoring, mesh-style networks, and projects where traditional WiFi falls short.

In this talk, Alex walked through what HaLow is, how it works, and where it fits alongside other wireless technologies.

`[ Slides & Materials`https://github.com/coredotradio/meetings/tree/main/meetings/2026-04]

